# GENERATED ARTIFACTS — never merged, always regenerated.
#
# `docs/backlog/INDEX.md` is generated by `tools/backlog-index.sh` from the
# per-lane files.  §9.5 moved the backlog's rebase collision off the §-numbers
# (where `L2`, `L3` and `L4` each appeared twice) and onto this one file: two
# lanes appending entries on the same day both regenerate it, and their diffs
# overlap.  The ES lane hit exactly that.
#
# TEXT-MERGING A GENERATED FILE IS ALWAYS WRONG — a line-merged index is a
# THIRD version that matches neither tree.  Resolving to ONE SIDE is also
# wrong, and that is what this driver used to do: measured, a rebase kept the
# ONTO side and dropped the rebasing lane's own rows, while a pull kept the
# branch and dropped the OTHER lanes' rows.  One driver, two directions, two
# different wrong answers, both silent.
#
# SO THE DRIVER MERGES THE ROWS.  Git hands a driver only the three versions of
# THIS file, never the merged sources — and it runs before the merged sources
# reach the working tree, so a driver that re-ran the generator would rebuild
# from the onto side and reproduce the drop.  Every row is present across the
# two sides, so the union of the rows is the correct merge.  Measured 4-of-4 in
# both directions where the old driver gave two different 3-of-4s.
#
# A retitle leaves a stale SUPERSET (old and new row), never a subset: nothing
# is lost and the next `tools/backlog-index.sh` regenerates it exactly.
# `tools/triad.sh`'s DOCS floor runs `--check`, so a stale index is refused
# rather than landed.
#
# THIS LINE ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH.  `ours` is NOT one of git's built-in merge
# drivers (those are `text`, `binary` and `union`); it must also be defined in
# each clone's config, or git falls back to a normal conflict.  Measured both
# ways.  Install it with:
#
#     tools/backlog-index.sh --install-merge-driver
#
# BUT NOBODY RUNS THAT.  Config is per-clone, and a fix that needs a human to
# type it is not a fix — measured, two conflicts in one day for one lane, and
# every lane pays it.  So `tools/triad.sh` and `tools/check.sh` call
# `tools/backlog-index.sh --ensure-driver` on their first run in a clone: if
# this file names a driver and the clone has not configured it, they configure
# it and say so in one line.  FIXES LIVE IN GATES.
#
# The driver is named for what it guards rather than `ours`, so a lane reading
# `git config --get-regexp '^merge\.'` sees WHY it is set.
#
docs/backlog/INDEX.md merge=backlog-index
